---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/tinyemail-vs-shopify-email';
const title = 'tinyEmail vs Shopify Email: current Shopify Messaging comparison';
const desc = 'Compare campaign production, welcome and cart exits, current cost inputs and migration ownership before choosing tinyEmail or Shopify Messaging.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Shopify Email still the current product name?",
    "answer": "Shopify now documents Shopify Messaging, formerly Shopify Email. This comparison keeps the familiar route but evaluates the current documented product rather than assuming the old email-only scope."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which should a new store choose?",
    "answer": "Start with the workflow you can operate reliably. Compare a branded campaign, welcome enrollment and a cart purchase exit. There is no order-volume threshold that forces a migration."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare cost?",
    "answer": "Use current account quotes for the same eligible contacts and send volume. Include included allowances, overages, paid features and operator time. Do not infer the bill from an old starting price."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I run both during migration?",
    "answer": "Use test recipients to validate the replacement first. Assign one production owner for each workflow and transfer it deliberately; parallel enrollment can duplicate welcome or cart messages."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I consider Sequenzy?",
    "answer": "It can be an email shortlist candidate if its current Shopify event support, suppression behavior and operating cost meet your requirements. This page does not establish a native integration, savings or a guaranteed graduation path."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should an AI content trial measure?",
    "answer": "Compare an approved draft with your product facts, brand voice and mobile layout. Record editing time and factual corrections. AI availability alone does not prove faster production or better conversion."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <div>
      <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">{desc}</p>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Current scope changes the decision</h2>
        <p>Shopify documentation now calls the product Shopify Messaging and describes multichannel marketing. Its campaign guide includes branded templates and custom code. tinyEmail advertises product sync, cart, welcome and win-back flows on its current website, while its app listing includes AI generation. These are supplier descriptions, not measured superiority. The old framing of a free native starter against an inevitably temporary paid tool is not a reliable decision rule.</p>
        <h2>Compare a campaign with your actual catalog</h2>
        <p>Build a proposed sample campaign containing a product with variants, an unavailable item and a long product title. Inspect product links, image cropping, mobile layout and sender details in each tool. Ask whether unavailable products are removed automatically or require editorial review. Shopify being native reduces one vendor relationship; a separate app may suit a preferred editor or operating process. Neither fact settles design quality without the actual draft.</p>
        <h2>Welcome enrollment is a state transition</h2>
        <p>Proposed test: submit a new subscriber, an existing subscriber and an email-only customer through the intended signup source. Check when each becomes eligible and whether an incentive is issued twice. Write a single owner for the form promise and the welcome send. A newsletter signup and a paid order are different triggers; do not let a form resubmission restart an introductory discount series.</p>
        <h2>Cart recovery needs purchase exits, not a claimed winner</h2>
        <p>Use an abandoned checkout followed by a completed purchase before the next queued message as a proposed acceptance case. Repeat with a changed cart and an unavailable variant. Inspect the actual trigger, delay, purchase exclusion and link destination. Do not assume either tool is limited to a single reminder or supports every desired branch because a marketing page says automation. Record what your account can build and the unresolved cases.</p>
        <h2>Pricing without an invented ceiling</h2>
        <p>Shopify publishes an email allowance and usage pricing; tinyEmail currently advertises a free Shopify offering. Read current eligibility, limits and account terms before calculating cost. Model campaign sends plus automated sends under both a normal month and a seasonal peak. Add paid features, any separate SMS charges and the staff hours required to produce and maintain campaigns. Avoid treating included sends as unlimited or assigning a fixed bill from a historical price table.</p>
        <h2>AI copy and founder time</h2>
        <p>A proposed creative trial should use the same product facts and approval standard in both tools. Record draft time, edits, unsupported claims removed and the final mobile review. Do not judge a tool by how quickly it generates unapproved copy. A founder who already has reusable approved content may value workflow clarity more than generation; a team with varied launches may value drafting assistance if it reduces total review work.</p>
        <h2>Migration with one production owner</h2>
        <p>Inventory active forms, campaigns and automated enrollment before moving contacts. Retain subscription status and available permission evidence; do not convert suppressed contacts into subscribers. Validate event timing and purchase exits with internal recipients, then transfer one flow at a time and disable its previous production trigger. Keep a rollback record. Running both welcome flows for a week is not a safe default, and no fixed migration duration is promised.</p>
        <h2>When another platform belongs on the shortlist</h2>
        <p>Expand the evaluation when a concrete requirement fails: shared SMS coordination, an external subscription event, a needed branch or manageable reporting. Bring that failed case to Klaviyo, Omnisend or another candidate rather than migrating at an arbitrary monthly order count. Sequenzy can be evaluated for the email role if it demonstrates the necessary data path and exclusions; no ranking, fixed pricing or strategy service is assumed here. Keep promotional SMS permissions separate from email enrollment.</p>
        <h2>Make the final decision reviewable</h2>
        <p>Record which workflows passed the proposed checks, who maintains them, current quotes and unresolved integration work. If both cover the requirements, compare total operating effort and switching cost. Revenue attribution alone does not establish incremental recovery; use a suitable holdout where feasible and do not claim an uplift from editor preference. This page reports no live merchant trial or performance result.</p>
        <h2>Primary evidence and limits</h2>
        <p>Checked November 25, 2026: <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging">Shopify Messaging scope</a>, <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/email/pricing/">Shopify email pricing</a>, <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/email/create-email/create-campaigns">Shopify campaign options</a>, <a class="link" href="https://www.tinyemail.com/">tinyEmail product description</a> and <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/tiny">tinyEmail listing</a>. Supplier descriptions support the narrow scope statements above; account-specific availability, deliverability and integration behavior remain untested.</p>
      </article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/tinyemail-vs-mailchimp">TinyEmail vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-shopify-email">Klaviyo vs Shopify Email</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-shopify-email">Sequenzy vs Shopify Email</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/print-on-demand">Email apps for artisan stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/welcome-series">Starter email playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="tinyEmail vs Shopify Messaging FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
